  abstract = {{$<$}jats:title{$>$}Abstract{$<$}/jats:title{$><$}jats:p{$>$}Prompted by the ubiquity of empirical observations of critical phenomena, often in non-equilibrium macrostates, we developed a modelling approach in which several critical phenomena coexist. Instead of a single critical point, many coexisting critical points in the system are identified, forming a one-dimensional critical manifold. Identified within our game-of-life-like heterogeneous agent-based simulation model, where agents can be created and annihilated in the presence of a catalyst, each critical point belonging to the critical manifold is associated with a multi-spectrum of critical exponents. We find this situation in non-equilibrium mixed percolation-like macrostates obeying continuous phase transitions. These macrostates are quasi-stationary, where some system characteristics are time-independent while others are not. This novel look at universality signals the existance of complexity of critical phenomena richer than described to date.{$<$}/jats:p{$>$}},
